  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 240 AM EDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nA Bermuda High will linger off the eastern seaboard, which will \ncontinue to spread southerly flow and low level moisture into the \nSoutheast. By the time the long term period picks up on Friday \nnight, dewpoints will be in the low to mid 70s and precipitable \nwater values will be between 1.75-2 inches across the majority of \nthe area. Meanwhile, a shortwave trough will be advancing east-\nnortheast from the middle Mississippi River Valley region towards \nthe lower Midwest. As this shortwave progresses, a weakening frontal \nboundary will be moving southward through central Tennessee and \napproaching the north Georgia border. Scattered to numerous \nthunderstorms are expected to continue through much of north Georgia \nto the south of this front. \n\nA warmer fetch of Gulf air will help high temperatures on Saturday \nrise into the upper 80s to low 90s in spite of increased cloud \ncoverage. The combination of diurnal destabilization and the \nboundary progressing further south will promote a further increase \nin thunderstorm coverage across the area by late Saturday morning \nand into the afternoon. PoPs at this time are forecast to range from \n75-85% in north Georgia (closer to the frontal boundary) and 50-70% \nin central Georgia. Given the high PWATs, stronger storms that \ndevelop will be efficient rainfall producers and could lead to \nlocalized flooding concerns, most likely in areas to the north of \nthe Fall Line. Shear ahead of the front will become negligible as \nthe front continues to weaken. Considering this and a nearly \nsaturated atmospheric column, the threat for organized severe \nweather will be low. However, with ample diurnal instability, a few \nstorms will be capable of becoming strong and capable of producing \nfrequent lightning and gusty winds.\n\nSaturday night, the remnant boundary is anticipated to stall near \nthe I-85 corridor, with some showers expected to linger in the \nvicinity. With lesser precipitation and cloud coverage on Sunday, \ntemperatures are expected to increase by a couple of degrees, \nwarming into the low to mid 90s in all but the far northeastern \nmountains. With dewpoints remaining in the 70s, heat index values \nwill climb into the low to mid 100s in areas south of I-85 and in \nthe upper 90s in areas to the north in the peak heating hours. \nScattered afternoon pulse thunderstorms are expected on Sunday, \nthough there will be a local maximum (50-60% chance) in coverage \ncloser to the remnant boundary. There will be little change in the \nairmass during the early parts of next week, with humid conditions \nand above normal temperatures combining for heat indices exceeding \n100 across the majority of the area Monday through Wednesday. In an \nunstable airmass but in the absence of a surface forcing mechanism, \nisolated to widely scattered thunderstorms are expected each of \nthose afternoons.",
